<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guidobaldo II della Rovere</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1514">1514</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guidobaldo II della Rovere was Duke of Urbino from 1538 to 1574. His first marriage, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulia-da-varano">Giulia da Varano</a>, gave him a claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> forced him to give it up in 1539. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> as a military commander and was a patron of the arts who commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>'s Venus of Urbino.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guidobaldo-ii-della-rovere.jpg" alt="Guidobaldo II della Rovere" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_(Tiziano_Vecellio)_-_Portrait_of_Guidobaldo_II_della_Rovere,_Duke_of_Urbino_-_1956.7.1_-_Yale_University_Art_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
